<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-limits-and-scalar-multiplication" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Limits and scalar multiplication
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m> \norm{ \oparg } </m> be a norm on a real or complex vector space <m> V </m>. Prove that
      
      <md>
        \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \alpha_{ n } \vect{ v }_{ n } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \alpha_{ n } } \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \vect{ v }_{ n } }
      </md>
      
      for any convergent sequences <m> \of{ \alpha_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> and <m> \of{ \vect{ v }_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of scalars <m> \alpha_{ n } </m> and vectors <m> \vect{ v }_{ n } \in V </m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m> \alpha = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \alpha_{ n } } </m> and <m> \vect{ v } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \vect{ v }_{ n } } </m>. Let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number.
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          0 \leq \norm{ \alpha_{ n } \vect{ v }_{ n } - \alpha \vect{ v } } \amp = \norm{ \alpha_{ n } \vect{ v }_{ n } - \alpha \vect{ v }_{ n } + \alpha \vect{ v }_{ n } - \alpha \vect{ v } } \leq \norm{ \alpha_{ n } \vect{ v }_{ n } - \alpha \vect{ v }_{ n } } + \norm{ \alpha \vect{ v }_{ n } - \alpha \vect{ v } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \norm{ \of{ \alpha_{ n } - \alpha } \vect{ v }_{ n } } + \norm{ \alpha \of{ \vect{ v }_{ n } - \vect{ v } } } = \abs{ \alpha_{ n } - \alpha } \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ n } } + \abs{ \alpha } \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ n } - \vect{ v } }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      <xref ref="proposition-sequential-convergence"/> and <xref ref="proposition-limits-and-vector-arithmetic"/> together imply that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \of{ \abs{ \alpha_{ n } - \alpha } \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ n } } + \abs{ \alpha } \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ n } - \vect{ v } } } } \amp = \of{ \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ \alpha_{ n } - \alpha } } } \of{ \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ n } } } } + \abs{ \alpha } \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ n } - \vect{ v } } } 
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = 0 \cdot \norm{ \vect{ v } } + \abs{ \alpha } \cdot 0 = 0 + 0 = 0
        </mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      In summary, we have shown that
      
      <md>
        0 \leq \norm{ \alpha_{ n } \vect{ v }_{ n } - \alpha \vect{ v } } \leq \abs{ \alpha_{ n } - \alpha } \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ n } } + \abs{ \alpha } \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ n } - \vect{ v } }
      </md>
      
      and that
      
      <md>
        \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \of{ \abs{ \alpha_{ n } - \alpha } \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ n } } + \abs{ \alpha } \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ n } - \vect{ v } } } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ 0 } = 0
      </md>.
      
      <xref ref="theorem-squeeze-theorem"/> implies that
      
      <md>
        \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \norm{ \alpha_{ n } \vect{ v }_{ n } - \alpha \vect{ v } } } = 0
      </md>,
      
      and so <m> \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \alpha_{ n } \vect{ v }_{ n } } = \alpha \vect{ v } </m> by <xref ref="proposition-sequential-convergence"/>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
